%description:

RFC 9293 — when the usable window turns negative, the sender writes no new data past the
right edge.

Checks RFC9293-WND-5 (should not, SHLD-15) from
doc/project/evidence/standard/rfc9293/catalog.md. The English procedure is in
doc/project/evidence/protocol/tcp/checks/window.md#no-new-data-past-a-shrunk-edge; the program steps below map
onto its expected observations. Mapping of the mockup with a relay: host A = host1, relay T =
tap, host B = host2.

The relay rewrites the window field of the fourth acknowledgment from host B to 100 octets
and computes the checksum again. By that time host A has several segments in flight, so the
right edge the relay writes falls behind the sequence number host A has already reached: the
usable window is negative, which is the state MUST-34 names. Host B itself never shrinks its
window; the relay is what a peer that does would look like from host A's side.

Expected result: PASS, since 2026-09-15. Until then this test failed and the failure was
recorded as a **defect**. It was a **test error**, and the model obeys the rule.

What the trace shows. The shrunk acknowledgment reaches host A's TCP at t=0.2002652, host A
sets its send window to 100, and `TcpConnection::sendData` then writes
`Effective window is zero (advertised window 100, congestion window 2144), cannot send.`
Host A had 2320 octets buffered and a congestion window of 2144, so it wanted to send and did
not. That is SHLD-15 obeyed.

At t=0.20032412, 59 microseconds later, the next acknowledgment from host B arrives carrying
a window of 7504, because host B never shrank anything: only the relay did, and only on one
segment. Host A's window reopens and it sends 2144 octets. That send is lawful, and the old
watch caught it, because the watch ran for 0.3 seconds -- five thousand times the interval
the rule governs. RFC 9293 states the rule for the state it names: *if* the usable window is
negative, the sender should not send new data. Once the peer advertises a larger window the
usable window is positive again and the rule no longer applies.

The watch now runs for 50 microseconds, which sits inside the 59-microsecond interval during
which the shrunk edge stands. A model that broke the rule would send in the same event as the
shrunk acknowledgment, at the first instant of that window, which is what the model's own
refusal happens in. The window is tied to the timing of this scenario, so a change to the
link or to the segment sizes needs it measured again.

See doc/project/evidence/model/tcp/results.md.

The companion test Rfc9293ShrunkWindow.test carries the other half of the same event, that
the sender survives the shrinking, and it passes. The two are apart so that this failure
cannot hide that verdict.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "TcpMutations.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("rfc9293_shrunk_window_no_new_data")
        // The relay shrinks the window of the fourth acknowledgment from host B.
        .intercept(tap("tap").filterPacket("tcp.srcPort == 1000 && tcp.ackBit == true").nth(4)
                     .mutate([](Packet *frame) {
                         rewriteTcpHeader(frame, [](TcpHeader& h) { h.setWindow(100); });
                     })
                     .describe("host B's fourth acknowledgment (shrink its window to 100 octets)"))
        // Observation 1: host A sends its SYN. Record ISS_A.
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == true && tcp.ackBit == false")
                  .capture("issA", "tcp.sequenceNo")
                  .describe("host A's SYN, carrying ISS_A").within(0.5))
        // Observation 2: the shrunk acknowledgment reaches host A. Record where it says the
        // stream stands; the new right edge is that number plus 100.
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetReceivedFromLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.ackBit == true && tcp.window == 100")
                  .capture("una", "tcp.ackNo")
                  .describe("the shrunk acknowledgment at host A, advertising 100 octets")
                  .within(1.0))
        // Observation 3: host A sends no new data past the new right edge (RFC9293-WND-5). A
        // retransmission of data the peer has not acknowledged starts at the left edge and is
        // therefore not caught by this watch, which is right: the document allows it.
        //
        // The watch sits at host A's TCP and not at its interface. The interface signals a
        // send when the transmission ends, which can fall after an acknowledgment that
        // arrived while the frame was on the wire; the module signals when it decides to
        // send, which is the moment this rule is about. A segment is told from a bare
        // acknowledgment by its own length: a header alone is 20 octets.
        .never(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.sequenceNo >= {una} + 100")
                  .filterEvent([](const MatchContext& c) { return c.event.packet->getByteLength() > 20; })
                  .describe("a segment of new data past the shrunk right edge")
                  // The interval the rule governs: it opens when the shrunk advertisement
                  // arrives and closes 59 microseconds later, when host B advertises 7504
                  // again. See the description.
                  .within(0.00005));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "rfc9293_shrunk_window_no_new_data compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;
import inet.protocoltest.PacketTap;

// The mockup with a relay of doc/project/evidence/protocol/tcp/checks.md#common-mockups:
// host A -- relay T -- host B.
network Rfc9293TapNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        host1: StandardHost { @display("p=250,100"); }
        tap: PacketTap { @display("p=400,100"); }
        host2: StandardHost { @display("p=550,100"); }
    connections:
        host1.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> tap.a;
        tap.b <--> Eth100M <--> host2.ethg++;
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = Rfc9293TapNet
sim-time-limit = 10s
seed-set = 0

# Static ARP keeps the wire free of ARP request/reply noise.
**.ipv4.arp.typename = "GlobalArp"

# Every node computes real TCP checksums (checks.md's common mockup constant: the checksum
# is never optional, MUST-2). The relay computes them again after every change it makes.
**.checksumMode = "computed"

# The scenario constants of checks.md#common-mockups. Host B only receives: echoFactor = 0
# keeps the reverse direction free of data, so that the segments from host B are pure
# acknowledgments and an observation about them cannot be confused with an echo.
*.host1.numApps = 1
*.host1.app[0].typename = "TcpSessionApp"
*.host1.app[0].connectAddress = "host2"
*.host1.app[0].connectPort = 1000
*.host1.app[0].tOpen = 0.1s
*.host1.app[0].tSend = 0.2s
*.host1.app[0].sendBytes = 5000B
*.host1.app[0].tClose = 0.4s
*.host2.numApps = 1
*.host2.app[0].typename = "TcpEchoApp"
*.host2.app[0].localPort = 1000
*.host2.app[0].echoFactor = 0

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc9293_shrunk_window_no_new_data: PASS
